+    Decays from ``base_lr`` at round 0 to exactly ``base_lr * 0.01`` at the
+    final round (``total_rounds - 1``) following the standard cosine schedule:
+
+        lr(t) = lr_min + 0.5 * (lr_max - lr_min) * (1 + cos(╧Ç * t / (T - 1)))
+
+    The denominator is ``T - 1`` (not ``T``) because the loop in main.py runs
+    ``for rnd in range(total_rounds)``, so ``round_num`` ranges over the closed
+    integer set {0, 1, ΓÇª, T-1}.  Using T-1 as denominator maps this domain
+    exactly onto the cosine interval [0, ╧Ç]:
+
+        t = 0   ΓåÆ cos(0)   = +1  ΓåÆ lr = base_lr          (maximum)
+        t = T-1 ΓåÆ cos(╧Ç)   = -1  ΓåÆ lr = lr_min = base_lr * 0.01  (minimum)
+
+    Using T instead (the original formula) maps the domain onto [0, ╧Ç(T-1)/T],
+    which reaches only ~╧Ç - ╧Ç/T at the final round.  With T=100 this leaves
+    the LR ~2.5 % above lr_min on the last round ΓÇö never reaching the target
+    minimum.
+
+    Parameters
